{"authored" [{"question" "q-69950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "65c7445f65130fe4c828044f5045b651566096bf51c03481ccccb54865a65ef6" "basis" 1470 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-69950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $3$rd row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+2\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "65c7445f65130fe4c828044f5045b651566096bf51c03481ccccb54865a65ef6" "basis" 1470 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-69950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23d57ef5d320" "basis" 1470 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A